e('The handful of mistakes behind most real cryptographic failures','Common mistakes that cause cryptographic failures')
e('When cryptography fails in production, it is almost always because a correct primitive was used in the wrong way:','Common causes of production cryptographic failure include')
e('ECB mode encrypts each 16-byte block independently','ECB mode encrypts each block independently (16 bytes for AES, 8 for 3DES)')
e('Adobe\'s 2013 breach exposed about 150 million passwords encrypted with 3DES in ECB mode: identical passwords had identical ciphertexts, and users\' password hints did the rest.','For password verification, deterministic encryption would reveal repeated values; store salted password hashes instead.\n\n> [!note] Content gap\n> Exact Adobe breach counts, its cipher attribution, generic GPU cracking rates and attack-runtime estimates are omitted where this review did not obtain adequate primary evidence. The examples below describe the cryptographic mechanisms without those unsupported specifics.')
e('After observing 624 consecutive 32-bit outputs, an attacker can reconstruct its state and predict every future "random" token.','After obtaining a suitable consecutive sequence of 624 full 32-bit MT outputs, an attacker can reconstruct state and predict later generator outputs. Tokens produced through rejection sampling or truncated outputs require a different observation/recovery analysis.')
e('Every SSH and TLS key generated on Debian and Ubuntu for two years came from a set of roughly 32,768 possibilities per key type.','Affected OpenSSL-generated keys became predictable; the key space depended on process IDs, architecture and generation details. It was not one universal set of 32,768 keys across every key type and platform.')
e('Comparing a received MAC with `==` stops at the first differing byte. By measuring response time, an attacker can learn how many leading bytes are right and forge a valid tag a byte at a time.','Ordinary equality has no constant-time security guarantee. An early-exit comparison can reveal prefix information in suitable timing conditions, but Python byte equality is not a guaranteed one-byte-at-a-time oracle. Use the dedicated comparison API and validate expected types and lengths.')
e('    # constant-time: compares every byte','    # avoids content-based short circuit')
e('A GPU computes billions of SHA-256 hashes per second. Use a password hash: Argon2id, scrypt or bcrypt.','Fast general-purpose hashes make offline guessing cheap relative to purpose-built password hashing. Use appropriately configured Argon2id or scrypt; bcrypt is a legacy option with input-length constraints.')
e('JWT `alg: none` and TLS downgrades both let the message decide how it is verified.','Trusting an unvalidated JWT algorithm field can bypass verification. Enforce the expected algorithm and key type. TLS negotiation is normal; downgrade vulnerabilities arise when version/suite policy and transcript binding fail.')
e('High-level APIs like libsodium\'s `crypto_secretbox` / `crypto_box`, Google\'s Tink, or `cryptography`\'s Fernet choose the algorithm, generate nonces and authenticate for you.','High-level APIs can reduce misuse, but responsibilities differ. libsodium\'s `crypto_secretbox` and `crypto_box` require caller-supplied unique nonces; `secretstream` manages stream nonce/state. Tink AEAD APIs and Fernet manage their own encryption randomness. Fernet is authenticated encryption but has no caller-provided AAD interface.')
e('| AES-256, SHA-256 | Grover | Still secure |','| AES-256 | Grover key search | Idealised ~2^128 oracle queries; practical costs are higher |\n| SHA-256 | Quantum search/collision algorithms | Preimage and collision security differ; do not assign one universal bit strength |')
e('so every public-key algorithm in this module falls.','so the classical factoring/discrete-log public-key schemes discussed earlier are vulnerable. The post-quantum schemes below rely on different assumptions.')
e('Estimates range from about ten years to never.','A reliable arrival date cannot be supplied here; planning uses explicit scenarios rather than a forecast.')
e('**Signatures** are less urgent: a forged signature is only useful while it is being checked, so they need replacing before the machine exists, not years before.','**Signatures** also need early migration planning: long-lived devices, software-update trust anchors and archived signatures can outlast a transition. Recorded-traffic confidentiality and future authentication are distinct risks; one universal priority order is inappropriate.')
e('SIKE in about an hour on a single laptop core.','the SIKEp434 parameter set in about an hour on a single core in the original attack authors\' reported implementation.')
e('| X25519 | 32 B | 32 B |','| X25519 | 32 B | 32 B peer public share (not a ciphertext/signature) |')
e('ML-KEM is also *fast*, often faster than X25519, so the cost is bandwidth, not CPU.','ML-KEM can be computationally efficient, but relative CPU cost depends on implementation, platform and operation. Key and ciphertext size are additional deployment costs, not the only ones.')
e('most deployments are **hybrid**','several prominent deployments use **hybrid** key exchange')
e('so the result is secure if either holds.','so a correctly analysed combiner can preserve security if either component holds; whole-process compromise is outside that guarantee.')
e('`X25519MLKEM768` is on by default in major browsers and CDNs; it adds about 1.1 KB to each direction of the handshake.','Chrome introduced `X25519MLKEM768` in version 131. Relative to X25519 alone, its key-share payload adds 1,184 bytes client-to-server and 1,088 bytes server-to-client; deployment policies vary.')
e('A TLS chain carries two public keys and three signatures (two in certificates, one in CertificateVerify), plus transparency proofs. With ML-DSA that is around 10 to 15 KB instead of about 1 KB, enough to push handshakes past TCP\'s initial congestion window and add a round trip.','For an illustrative leaf-plus-one-intermediate chain, two public keys and three signatures are transmitted, including CertificateVerify. At ML-DSA-65 sizes these alone total 13,831 bytes, before certificate encoding/extensions and any SCTs. Actual chain composition and TCP initial-window configuration determine whether more acknowledgement pacing is needed; an extra full round trip is not automatic.')
e('This is usually the longest step.','Inventory and dependencies can dominate the migration work.')
e('**Deploy hybrid key exchange now**, which is mostly a library upgrade.','**Evaluate supported hybrid key exchange** through compatibility testing, library/service upgrades and deployment policy; middleboxes, protocol negotiation and operational constraints may also require changes.')
e('NIST has proposed deprecating RSA and elliptic-curve algorithms around 2030 and disallowing them by 2035.','the November 2024 draft NIST IR 8547 proposed security-strength-dependent transition dates, including 2030 deprecation and 2035 disallowance for listed classical uses. Treat this as a dated draft proposal, not a universal law or a final rule for every application.')
e('Most crypto failures are misuse:','Common cryptographic misuse includes')
f(1,'workedExample','MT19937 is not a cryptographic generator. A suitable sequence of624 full32-bit outputs allows state cloning; random.choice tokens expose partial outputs through rejection sampling, so that simple count is not an exact recovery guarantee for this API. Predictability risk is sufficient reason to use secrets.token_urlsafe(32), backed by the operating system CSPRNG.')
f(2,'prompt','A hypothetical leaked password database uses3DES in ECB mode under one shared key and includes plaintext password hints. Why does encryption still reveal useful information to an attacker?')
f(2,'workedExample','ECB maps equal plaintext blocks to equal ciphertext blocks under the same key. Repeated padded passwords can therefore be grouped; hints may make those groups easier to guess without recovering the key. For login verification, use a salted password hash. Applications that genuinely need to retrieve an external password have a different secret-storage requirement.')
f(3,'explanation','Ordinary equality is not a constant-time API. Its timing behavior depends on implementation; do not rely on it for secret-tag comparison.',0)
f(3,'explanation','Correct. It avoids content-based short-circuit comparison; types and lengths can still affect behavior, so validate the expected tag representation.',3)
f(3,'workedExample','An idealised bytewise early-exit comparison can leak the matching-prefix length, but a real attack depends on timing signal, implementation and repeated samples. There is no universal32×256-request upper bound for Python equality. hmac.compare_digest is the appropriate content-independent comparison API for validated equal-type/equal-length tags.')
f(4,'text','The overlapping p2 bytes; GCM nonce reuse also compromises authentication and can enable tag forgery',1)
f(4,'explanation','Correct. XOR cancels the repeated keystream. Repeated-nonce tags give equations involving the authentication subkey; recovery and forgery depend on available messages/tags and nonce masks.',1)
f(4,'explanation','The AES key is not recovered by this XOR attack. Authentication-subkey recovery and tag forgery are separate consequences with message-dependent conditions.',3)
f(5,'explanation','Correct for the raw secret-prefix SHA-256 construction when key length is known or guessed. Whether the forged byte string is accepted as a useful request also depends on parser and format validation.',2)
f(6,'text','Using a correctly implemented AEAD API that withholds unauthenticated plaintext and uniformly rejects invalid tags removes this CBC padding oracle',2)
f(6,'workedExample','An attacker varies preceding ciphertext/IV bytes and observes padding validity to constrain plaintext. False positives and message format affect query count; no universal128-queries-per-byte guarantee applies. A padding oracle can also support chosen-plaintext ciphertext construction when the IV/format permits. Correct AEAD avoids CBC padding altogether and must not release unauthenticated plaintext; encrypt-then-MAC must verify before CBC decryption.')
f(7,'text','Under this hypothetical timeline,25+7=32 exceeds15 by17years, indicating a migration urgency window rather than17years of measured historical lateness',0)
f(7,'explanation','Correct. The arithmetic is a planning model, not a forecast. Data protected only by vulnerable classical key establishment could remain sensitive after the assumed quantum capability arrives.',0)
f(8,'explanation','Shor does not directly break AES or SHA-256. Grover gives an idealised square-root search advantage; SHA-256 preimage and collision analyses are different security questions.',0)
f(9,'explanation','This ignores the stated ML-DSA sizes. A P-256 comparison would also need explicit public-key encoding and signature sizes, plus certificate metadata.',2)
f(9,'workedExample','Two public keys:2×1,952=3,904bytes. Three signatures:3×3,309=9,927bytes. Total13,831bytes. With a configured ten-segment initial window and1,460-byte MSS, the nominal window is14,600bytes; other handshake bytes can exceed it. Additional pacing depends on actual window, ACK behavior and the rest of the flight, so a full extra RTT is not guaranteed.')
f(10,'prompt','Why might a deployment choose hybrid X25519MLKEM768 instead of ML-KEM alone? Select all that apply.')
f(10,'explanation','Correct for a flaw confined to the PQ component without exposing the classical secret or shared process state. A memory-safety bug compromising both components is not contained by hybrid mathematics.',1)
f(10,'explanation','Correct under the specified combiner assumptions and correct implementations; it is not a guarantee against endpoint compromise.',2)
f(10,'explanation','A hybrid performs both operations and does not avoid ML-KEM work. Relative CPU speeds vary; larger payloads are an additional consideration.',3)
f(10,'workedExample','Hybrids hedge against failure of one cryptographic component, under the combiner assumptions. The original SIKEp434 attack authors reported about one hour on a single core; that is a particular measured implementation, not a universal runtime. Implementation bugs still matter: a bug exposing both secrets defeats the hedge. Authentication/signature migration remains separate.')
f(11,'explanation','With one shared key, two files encrypted in the same second reuse a nonce. Independently generated per-file keys change that analysis, but timestamp-only nonce generation is unsafe for a reused key.',3)
f(12,'explanation','Shor needs a sufficiently capable quantum computer for its efficient factoring advantage. Classical GCD can reveal a shared prime without factoring either modulus independently; timing depends on input and implementation.',2)
f(12,'workedExample','If n1=p×q1 and n2=p×q2 with distinct other primes, gcd(n1,n2)=p. Dividing yields the remaining factors. Batch-GCD techniques scale this check to collections of public keys. Insufficient entropy can cause shared factors; a universal microsecond or multi-hour runtime is omitted because no benchmark setup is specified.')
